    fn __data33() -> Py { py_dict(vec![(py_str("birdwatching"), py_str("Go out with binoculars borrowed from a friend or a reserve's visitor centre first, and start with the birds in your own street: the RSPB and the Woodland Trust both have a garden bird sheet for free. A pair of 8x42 binoculars for about a hundred pounds is the one purchase, second hand is fine, and early morning in spring is when everything is singing.")), (py_str("photography"), py_str("Use the phone you already have for a month and finish one project - a colour, a street, one tree every day - before you buy a camera. Then a second-hand body with a single 35mm or 50mm lens beats a new camera with two bad ones. Learn three things first: light behind you, get closer than feels natural, and take ten shots of one thing instead of one shot of ten things.")), (py_str("knitting"), py_str("A pair of 4mm needles and a ball of smooth aran wool is the whole kit, about ten pounds, and a plain square is the first project. Watch one slow video of knit and purl rather than reading about it, make a dishcloth, and only then start a scarf. Wool shops and libraries both run free sit-and-knit groups, which is how most people get past the first mess.")), (py_str("pottery"), py_str("Book a one-off taster session before you buy anything: most cities have a studio that runs two-hour wheel evenings for about thirty pounds. If you like it, a six-week course teaches more than a year of videos, and hand-building at home needs only clay, a board and a cheap kiln share from the same studio.")), (py_str("woodworking"), py_str("Start with one joint and one small thing - a box, a stool - using hand tools: a hardpoint saw, a chisel, a square and a bench hook will do more than a garage of machines. Men's sheds and community workshops hire out bench time and somebody to ask, which is worth more than the tools when you are starting.")), (py_str("fishing"), py_str("In England and Wales you need a rod licence first - about seven pounds for a day, from the Environment Agency online - and then a day ticket at a local club water and a beginner's float rod set for about forty pounds. Go with somebody who fishes if you can, and learn one knot (the improved clinch), one rig and one bait before buying anything else. Go in the morning in summer: fishing is mostly about being somewhere quiet, so the first trip is a success if you enjoy it.")), (py_str("climbing"), py_str("Go to an indoor wall with hired shoes and a taster session first, about twenty pounds, and try bouldering: it needs no partner and the routes are the height of a room. Give it three visits before you buy shoes, and only then a pair that fits tightly. Outdoors comes later, with somebody who already knows how to belay.")), (py_str("painting"), py_str("Start with acrylics: they dry in minutes, clean up with water, and a starter set is under twenty pounds. If it is watercolour you want, the paper decides everything - cold-pressed 300gsm and no cheaper - and you work light to dark because you cannot paint white back in. Either way, paint the same simple thing - one mug, one window - five times instead of five different things, and work bigger than feels comfortable with one brush that holds a point.")), (py_str("singing"), py_str("Find a choir rather than a teacher first: most towns have a community choir that takes people who have never sung, no audition, about five pounds a session. Singing with others teaches pitch faster than singing alone, and the confidence is most of it. Take a teacher later if you want to sing on your own.")), (py_str("dancing"), py_str("Pick a class that runs a free taster and go with no partner - most salsa, swing and ceroc nights rotate partners anyway. Wear smooth soles and give it three weeks before deciding, because the first lesson is always clumsy. A weekly class beats a course of videos, since the whole thing is other people's timing.")), (py_str("baking"), py_str("Weigh 500g strong white flour, 7g dried yeast, 10g salt and 350ml warm-not-hot water: that is the whole recipe, and a scale is the only kit you need. Knead ten minutes, leave it two hours somewhere warm until it has doubled, shape it and bake it hot. Then bake that same loaf five times rather than five different things - the first is usually dense and the fifth is usually good, so the only way to fail is to stop at the first.")), (py_str("sewing"), py_str("A basic machine with a straight stitch and a zigzag is all anyone needs, and a second-hand one from a repair shop costs less than a new cheap one. Start on scraps, not clothes: stitch straight lines, then a cushion cover, then take in a hem on something you do not mind ruining. A local sewing shop's two-hour beginner class is worth more than a video, because someone sets the tension for you.")), (py_str("board games"), py_str("Go to a board-game cafe or a club night once before buying a box: most of them have a shelf and someone who will teach a game in ten minutes. Then buy one small game for the people you actually play with, not the one with the best reviews. Codenames, Patchwork and Ticket to Ride cover most groups.")), (py_str("yoga"), py_str("Book a beginners' class rather than a video: most studios run a drop-in hatha or beginner class for about ten to fifteen pounds, and a teacher can see your back, which a video cannot. Wear something you can move in and borrow a mat - a cheap 6mm mat is the only thing worth buying - and go twice a week for a month before you judge it. Skip any pose that hurts and tell the teacher about a bad back or neck before you start.")), (py_str("pilates"), py_str("Start with a beginner mat class, not the reformer: a mat class is about ten to fifteen pounds, needs only a mat and a cushion, and a teacher who watches you is the point of it. The one thing to learn first is the breath - wide into the ribs on the effort, with the belly gently held, never sucked in hard. Twice a week for a month, and tell the teacher about any back or neck trouble beforehand."))]) }
